LAB 04: Data Types, Mutability, and Collections

Course: Software Construction (5th Semester Software Engineering)  
Institution: University of Engineering and Technology, Abbottabad Campus  
Date: September 9, 2026  

📌 Title & Objective
This repository contains the implementation of **Lab Task 04: Data Types, Mutability, and Collections**. The main objective of this lab is to explore and apply fundamental concepts of object mutability, collection interface implementations (`List`, `Map`, `Set`), runtime immutability wrappers, safe collection traversal using iterators, and designing custom immutable classes in Java.

🛠️ Tasks Implemented

Lab Tasks
Lab Task 1: Mutability & Performance (`StringPerformance.java`)
Implemented `buildString(int n)` using standard string concatenation (`+` operator) to demonstrate $O(n^2)$ time complexity.
Implemented `buildStringBuilder(int n)` using `StringBuilder.append()` to demonstrate amortized $O(n)$ performance efficiency for $n = 10000$.

Lab Task 2: Safe Collection Modification (`CourseManager.java`)
Demonstrated how modifying an `ArrayList` during a standard `for-each` loop throws a `ConcurrentModificationException`.
Resolved the runtime exception using an explicit `Iterator` and `iter.remove()` to safely filter course codes starting with `"6."`, leaving `["8.03", "14.03"]`.

Lab Task 3: Complex Data Types (`TreasureMap.java`)
Stored key-value treasure coordinates in a `HashMap<String, Double>`.
Dynamically updated map entries based on current collection size and iterated over `treasures.values()` using a `for-each` loop to compute the total sum.

Lab Task 4: Unmodifiable Wrappers (`Zoo.java`)
Created a collection wrapper using `Collections.unmodifiableList()`.
Verified runtime immutability by catching an `UnsupportedOperationException` when attempting to execute `.add("flamingo")`.

Homework Tasks
Homework 1: Enhanced Map Tracking (`StudentDirectory.java`)
Implemented a student tracking system mapping IDs to student names.
Encapsulated map keys by returning an unmodifiable set via `Collections.unmodifiableSet(map.keySet())` and verified that illegal modifications throw an `UnsupportedOperationException`.

Homework 2: Designing Immutable Classes (`Point.java`)
Created a completely immutable `Point` class using `final` class and field modifiers (`int x`, `int y`) with no setter methods.

🧠 Homework 2 Reflection: Immutability Principles

Why Immutable `Point` is Safer from Bugs and Easier to Understand:
Making the `Point` class immutable guarantees that its internal state (`x` and `y`) can never be modified after instantiation. 
